  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1010 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nTonight and Tuesday...\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis this evening depicts a \nbroad mid- to upper-level ridge over the central and western\nCONUS, keeping conditions calm locally. A cold front pushed \nsouthward across the area this afternoon, shifting low-level \nflow from southerly to northerly. Clear skies overnight will \nallow low temperatures to dip into the mid 30s to low 40s. Calm \nwinds overnight may also bring patchy areas of fog. However, \nmodel soundings depict a very shallow near-saturated layer near \nthe surface, likely limiting fog to patchy and low-lying areas. \n\nOtherwise, expect daybreak to bring another calm and mostly sunny \nday. Low-level flow will transition back to southerly, pushing high \ntemperatures into the low to mid 60s, a slight decrease from \nprevious days but still approximately 10 degrees above average for \nearly November. \n\nWednesday through Friday...\n\nTuesday night into Wednesday, a shortwave disturbance will eject \ninto the northern Plains and get swept into an amplifying trough \nover the eastern 2/3rds of the CONUS. In response, a surface low is \nexpected to develop across the northern Plains, traversing to the \nsoutheast and pulling into central Iowa by early Wednesday. Moisture \nwill be fairly limited with this system as it traverses our area, \nincreasing as it pulls east into Iowa, limiting PoPs locally. \nHowever, an increasing pressure gradient and strong northwesterly \nflow on the back side of the system will help usher in cooler air to \nthe region through the remainder of the work week. High temperatures \non Wednesday are expected to peak in the 50s, with brisk \nnorthwesterly wind gusts of 25-35 mph.\n\nAs cooler air continues to filter into the area, high temperatures \nwill drop into the low to mid 40s on Thursday and Friday. Low \ntemperatures Thursday morning are expected to dip into the 30s, with \ntemperatures near freezing across northeast Nebraska. Friday looks \nto be the coolest morning of the week, with widespread lows in the \nmid- to upper-20s. Omaha will likely see it's first freezing \ntemperatures of the season Friday morning, which would tie our \nrecord for the latest first freeze set back in 2015. Another shot at \nfreezing temperatures, with some locations dipping back into \nthe upper 20s, will be present Saturday morning.\n\nSaturday and Beyond...\n\nBy this weekend, a narrow corridor of mid-level ridging will work \ninto the central Plains, introducing a warming trend. Highs are \nexpected to peak in the upper 40s Saturday, low 50s Sunday, and \nupper 50s on Monday and Tuesday. Long range guidance depicts a \nmid- level low traversing the southern into central Plains late \nSaturday into Sunday. While the bulk of deterministic long range\nguidance keeps this feature just south of the forecast area, \napproximately 1/3rd of GEFS and EC/EC-AIFS ensemble members \ntrack this feature further north, bringing precipitation chances\n(and a few members with light snow) to the area. We'll have to \nwatch how this feature trends as it approaches, with PoPs of \n15-30% in place south of Interstate-80 for the time being. The \nlatest forecast package keeps all precipitation as rain, which \nappears reasonable given the limited cooling expected to keep \ntemperatures just above freezing into Sunday morning. \n\nLooking ahead, a brief period of ridging and mild temperatures will \nreturn to start the next work week before a more amplified flow \npattern and additional precipitation chances return by mid-week.",
